vsnprintf parses the whole conversion specification, not just its last letter.

WHAT WAS WRONG. The format loop read the character after `%` and compared it
against six conversions. A flag, a width, a precision or a length modifier
matched none of them, so it emitted nothing for the `%`, advanced one
character, and copied the remainder of the specification out as literal text.

    "#define __TINYC__ 9%.2s\n", &TCC_VERSION[4]      tccpp.c:3631

became `#define __TINYC__ 92s`. `92s` is not a number, so every `#if __TINYC__`
in tcc's own test suite failed:

    tests/tcctest.c:337: error: invalid number

AND IT DID NOT CONSUME THE ARGUMENT. That is the worse half, because it is
silent. An unrecognised specification fell through without calling va_arg, so
every conversion after it in the same format string read the WRONG SLOT and
printed a plausible number from someone else's value. tcc uses 41 `%lx`, 24
`%p`, 12 `%-2d`, 11 `%08x` and 11 `%04x` among others -- none of which this
understood -- so any diagnostic mixing them was misaligned from the first one
onward, with nothing to say so.

WHAT IS IMPLEMENTED NOW. The full shape:

    %[flags][width][.precision][length]conversion

flags `-` `0` `+` ` ` `#`; width and precision as digits or `*`; length
modifiers h/hh/l/ll/z/j/t/L; conversions d i u o x X c s p %%.

The length modifier is REMEMBERED rather than skipped. Where int and long are
the same width -- true of the compiler this is built by -- fetching everything
as a long is exact. Where they are not it is not: an int argument occupies a
slot whose upper half means nothing, and reading it as a long turned -42 into
4294967254. Keeping the flag costs one variable and makes the same source
correct under both, which is what let it be tested against glibc at all.

WHAT IS NOT. e E f g G a A n -- there is no floating point here to convert
from. They emit the specification verbatim AND consume one slot, so what
follows stays aligned: a visibly wrong field instead of an invisible shift of
everything after it.

THE RESUME CONTRACT IS UNCHANGED, WHICH IS WHY THE ROOM CHECK MOVED. vfprintf
calls this in PRINTF_BUFFER_SIZE chunks and restarts from
__vsnprintf_string_offset with __vsnprintf_ap. So "does it fit" is now asked
once per specification instead of once per character, and on truncation the
offset points at the START of that specification with the va_list snapshotted
from before its arguments were taken. Previously a long `%s` could be cut in
half and its remainder dropped -- the offset had already advanced past it. A
specification too large for an empty buffer is emitted truncated rather than
deferred, because otherwise vfprintf would ask for it forever.

`__vsnprintf_ap = arg` requires an assignable va_list. M2libc's is; glibc's is
an array type. The original code already relied on this, so it is not a new
constraint -- but it is why the test harness below copies with va_copy.

TESTED AGAINST glibc, NOT AGAINST ITSELF. tools/test-vsnprintf.sh compiles this
function with gcc under the name m2_vsnprintf and compares its output with the
platform snprintf over every conversion tcc actually uses, the width and
precision forms, `*` width and precision, and three argument-alignment probes
of the shape that used to fail silently. 50 checks. It runs in about a second
and needs nothing but gcc, so it can gate.

    before  4 failures, all negative %d      (the int-vs-long fetch above)
    after   50 checks, 0 failures

AND IN THE PIPELINE:

    predef line, before   #define __TINYC__ 92s
    predef line, after    #define __TINYC__ 928
    gcc-built control     #define __TINYC__ 928

    tcctest.c before      tests/tcctest.c:337: error: invalid number
    tcctest.c after       past 337; stops later, in the allocator:
                          "M2libc: realloc: pointer was never returned by
                           malloc" -- which is patch 0006 doing its job

NO REGRESSION. difftest aarch64 79/79, amd64 77/77, stage-2 corpus 423/426 --
the same rows as before.

